#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d67d90e-3086-5c33-81a1-837122dde83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d67d90e-3086-5c33-81a1-837122dde83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281070} :answer-field/id #uuid "e984ea20-20a2-525f-bb72-465ba18386e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a86fb535-4f8d-57c1-9fa1-bf1be04d8909" :question/math-academy-id "q-167778" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that \n\n$$\n\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nApplying this formula and the constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4346e5c-bdc4-5d4f-8af1-6038fbb7153a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4346e5c-bdc4-5d4f-8af1-6038fbb7153a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281073} :answer-field/id #uuid "d68da75e-5f94-54c9-b18b-ec0320a16ca3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57e92349-31fa-5022-8e13-de0291dfd21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167779" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the second derivative, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ccb93c-d647-572c-9f98-7edc257c9436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05ccb93c-d647-572c-9f98-7edc257c9436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281076} :answer-field/id #uuid "0868508f-713d-5547-ab10-dcd68d1f014c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5bbfc16-b027-5d4d-9a8a-0cd8749b5376" :question/math-academy-id "q-167780" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6157d22-5b2c-5853-a924-21128765c154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6157d22-5b2c-5853-a924-21128765c154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281079} :answer-field/id #uuid "686f3271-5448-5dd1-8c71-c6977bdefa0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d45b5074-be70-5735-bec4-562dcb500b52" :question/math-academy-id "q-167781" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfc51d54-1410-5bb3-ad4c-c0ba242bcc9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfc51d54-1410-5bb3-ad4c-c0ba242bcc9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281082} :answer-field/id #uuid "1647f558-6e1e-53b6-8189-356141b9f320" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e101f5c-4c5d-5b21-abce-41e303609b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-167782" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8760269-bb4a-5737-88c9-6a6a6224b8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8760269-bb4a-5737-88c9-6a6a6224b8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281085} :answer-field/id #uuid "1feab918-8e04-5bd9-a78f-8dd01b24d30c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a97cda00-fc9d-5079-b510-4ceb0a789cf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167783" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a79f0c97-a8c2-536e-99f0-02c6cb4cbabf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a79f0c97-a8c2-536e-99f0-02c6cb4cbabf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281088} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad0fe185-a6a0-536d-a89d-6fa95735a4ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281087} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be97a0e-bf3e-57b0-84c4-f2724f00d847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3be97a0e-bf3e-57b0-84c4-f2724f00d847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281090} :answer-field/id #uuid "f492c6f2-acd8-5999-a66f-0e10dee5f35c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2eeacf14-b834-550d-8379-24e6ee1b286f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167784" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | $2\\cos(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/assets/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ when $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A stationary point of $g(x)$ is a value of $x$ where ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=-2$ and $x=2,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at these points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/assets/36fd58470a5361250fa7852d92abe2b9c2dbf18ca97d00e51ea4013f8f3aa348.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates (in ascending order) are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cae5c1b-57bd-51f6-a360-db946d25532f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "561f9027-2496-5ab7-aa39-2bbc03c048b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "561f9027-2496-5ab7-aa39-2bbc03c048b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281093} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5ae061e-e08b-57e1-9273-f71df47623b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52c80da9-257e-59b9-93d5-8335b5eee908" :question/math-academy-id "q-167785" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | $2\\cos(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/assets/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ when $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fd2441f-38a5-56b3-81e4-218bdd545de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^x+12x^2-\\frac{x^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fd2441f-38a5-56b3-81e4-218bdd545de1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^x+12x^2-\\frac{x^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281096} :answer-field/id #uuid "c217bbda-9dd2-5100-8231-db46d73e6be5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "375f6e61-5cf7-5835-8041-278896ec7fac" :question/math-academy-id "q-167786" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | $2\\cos(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/assets/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ when $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nEarlier part 8:\n\n$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4\\cos(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84b2227e-420b-5715-8cca-48ed07d14cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84b2227e-420b-5715-8cca-48ed07d14cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281099} :answer-field/id #uuid "445fd96c-6a30-54e7-8cf9-7776c41becdb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49a8fef0-9677-5fe9-b194-d6a00296e3f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167787" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nCalculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=\\,\\,$ | ${e}^{x}($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\cos(x)-\\sin(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | $2\\cos(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980664/assets/5d2bfbefca5ce0a0b04accad183d0f43537d600c72000058b59f692398259a80.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-3,3]$ is plotted above. In ascending order, what are the $x$-coordinates of the points on this interval where ${g}^{′}(x)=0?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ when $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $x\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$\n\nEarlier part 8:\n\n$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4\\cos(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nEarlier part 9:\n\n$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^x+12x^2-\\frac{x^4}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConsider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x)\n$$\n\nThe order of this differential equation is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "We have the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$"}]}
